# Test-Driven Development

Tests are proof. For new behavior, write or update tests close to the change. For bugs, reproduce the bug with a failing test before fixing it when practical.

## When To Use

- New behavior or business logic.
- Bug fixes.
- Important user flows.
- Data transforms, validation, permissions, or integration boundaries.

Documentation-only and static template changes may use review/check scripts instead of product tests.

## Test Priorities

- Unit tests for pure logic.
- Integration tests for boundaries, database, APIs, and service adapters.
- End-to-end tests for critical user journeys.
- Browser/runtime checks for UI behavior.

## Test Quality

- Test behavior, not implementation details.
- Prefer real implementations or fakes over brittle mocks.
- Keep tests readable and descriptive.
- Avoid skipped tests unless clearly documented.

## Verification

Before claiming completion, report which tests/checks ran, their result, and any coverage gaps.
